{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the explosive rise of facebook and the turbulent personal relationships behind its creation. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is a tense, intelligent, and emotionally resonant drama that transcends its subject matter to become a profound commentary on ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as a brilliant but socially awkward prodigy whose obsession with validation and control drives him to build a global empire. his performance is nuanced, often cold and detached, yet strangely compelling. he makes zuckerberg feel real—not as a villain or hero, but as a complex human being shaped by rejection and intellectual arrogance.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally strong. andrew garfield brings vulnerability and heart as eduardo saverin, zuckerberg’s loyal best friend who becomes collateral damage in the pursuit of success. jesse plemons is quietly effective as the twin winklevoss brothers, whose legal battle with facebook underscores the film’s central theme: who truly owns an idea? and jude law, as the slick, morally ambiguous attorney for the plaintiffs, adds a layer of theatrical tension that never feels overdone.\n\naaron sorkin’s script is a marvel of rapid-fire dialogue, witty and dense with ideas. every line feels purposeful, every exchange layered with subtext. the courtroom scenes, framed as flashbacks, serve as a narrative device that keeps the story moving forward with relentless momentum. the structure is clever, using legal testimony to peel back the layers of a story that’s as much about friendship and loyalty as it is about technology.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is precise and atmospheric. the film’s visual style—cool tones, stark lighting, and tight compositions—mirrors the emotional distance between characters. the iconic score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and electronic, perfectly capturing the digital age’s cold beauty and underlying anxiety. the opening scene, set to the haunting melody of “handshake,” immediately establishes the tone: isolation disguised as connection.\n\nwhat makes the social network so enduring is its ability to feel both timely and timeless. it’s not just a movie about facebook—it’s about how the pursuit of power can erode human connection, how ideas are stolen and rebranded, and how the people who build the tools that connect us often end up the most alone. the final scene, where zuckerberg refreshes his ex-girlfriend’s facebook profile over and over, is devastating in its quiet loneliness.\n\nthe social network isn’t a documentary, and it doesn’t claim to be. it’s a fictionalized interpretation, but one that feels truer than many true stories. it’s a modern tragedy wrapped in the glossy packaging of a tech origin myth. by the end, you’re not sure whether to admire zuckerberg’s genius or pity his isolation—and that’s the brilliance of it. a flawless film, smart, stylish, and unforgettable.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.37157994508743286}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009815642843022943}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0058195204474031925}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00013195598148740828}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -1.2010457515716553}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.597391747054644e-05}, {"token": " explosive", "logprob": -1.7369593381881714}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.10027764737606049}, {"